BRL-CAD's Geometry Editing Library (LIBGED)
===========================================

LIBGED is a CLI library for working on BRL-CAD geometry.

LIBGED provides commands that may be invoked via C API or scripting
languages bound to the API.  It supports the following features:

* stateless command interface
* stateful commands via context structure (e.g., for objects & views)
* short and long versions of all flags
* automatic help generation for commands and flags
* TODO: automatic help flag recognition for -?, -h, and --help
* TODO: automatic detailed help for "help [command]"
* TODO: built-in globbing support
* TODO: transactional changes (all or nothing)
* TODO: built-in undo support
* TODO: command versioning

= Design =

LIBGED's CLI uses a consistent "{action} [args]" (i.e., VERB NOUN)
command pattern or "{obj_type} {action} [args]" for commands
specific to an object type (e.g., "attr show myobj").

LIBGED aims to present fewer than 100 top-level commands.

For development, the following guidelines are recommended best
practice for consistency and uniformity.

1. Usability first.  Helps to develop UI before doing implementation.

2. If something doesn't work well, deprecate, replace, and/or retire.

3. Maintainable means high cohesion, low coupling, & quick to modify.

4. All commands must be documented.  Docs help with scope and design.

5. Take features to completion, not perfection.  Iterate on feedback.

6. Coding solo can be satisfying, but often results in design poison.

7. Reduce, Reuse, Refactor; leverage existing; push duplication down.

Per ISO 9126-1, development priorities SHOULD typically be:
Usability > Reliability > Maintainability > Functionality > Efficiency

See TODO and include/ged.h for additional notes on LIBGED's design.

= Terminal width =

Hosts can call ged_set_terminal_columns before command execution to provide
available screen columns. The setting belongs to the GED session; zero restores
80 columns. Libged does not inspect COLUMNS, query a terminal, or install a resize
signal handler. Batch callers therefore retain a deterministic default. FLTK,
QGED, and MGED supply their console viewport width; gsh and classic MGED query
the stdout terminal. Changes take effect on subsequent commands, including
commands invoked by an interactive script.

The ordinary ls, tops, summary, and dup listings share the width-aware directory
formatter. Long names are preserved even when a single name exceeds the width.
Process listings use the session width for their existing line truncation.
Explicit ls formats (including -p and -l) and machine-readable command results
are unchanged. Scripts that parse ordinary column listings should request an
explicit format or split on whitespace rather than assume four columns.

Terminal wrapping and command formatting are separate: a terminal may wrap
incoming text without changing its bytes, but cannot reconstruct table cells
from old physical lines. Existing scrollback is not reformatted or rerun on a
resize. Matrices, analysis tables, material reports, and the legacy joint/button
column helpers still have their own layouts; changing these requires per-command
format decisions, especially for output consumed by scripts. The directory
formatter still measures names in bytes; full Unicode screen-cell accounting
is a separate concern from providing the current viewport width.

GED subprocess streams are pipes, rather than pseudo-terminals. They can be
wrapped by the host console but do not receive terminal dimensions or resize
events. Interactive external programs would need a pseudo-terminal transport
and corresponding resize forwarding to change their own output layout live.
